<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Citadel of the Ho Dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1397">1397</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Tay Do Citadel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudadela-teotihuacan">The Citadel</a> of the Ho Dynasty, or Tây Đô, is a stone citadel in Thanh Hóa province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, built in 1397 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-quy-ly">Hồ Quý Ly</a> as a new capital. Its walls are made of huge cut limestone blocks fitted without mortar. It served the short-lived Hồ dynasty until the Ming conquest of 1407 and was inscribed as a World Heritage Site in 2011.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/citadel-of-the-ho-dynasty.jpg" alt="Citadel of the Ho Dynasty" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: vi:Thành viên:Silviculture · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tay_Do_castle_South_gate.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
